C++ Hierarchical Inheritance – Vehicle, Car and Bike

Description

This C++ program demonstrates hierarchical inheritance.

In hierarchical inheritance, multiple derived classes inherit from the same base class.

Here:

- "Vehicle" is the base class.
- "Car" and "Bike" are derived classes.

Class Hierarchy

          Vehicle
          /     \
         ↓       ↓
       Car      Bike

1. Vehicle Class

The "Vehicle" class is the base class.

- "registrationNumber" stores the vehicle registration number.
- The constructor initializes the registration number.
- "start()" displays a message when the vehicle starts.
- "registrationNumber" is "protected", so it can be accessed by derived classes.

2. Car Class

The "Car" class inherits from "Vehicle".

class Car : public Vehicle

It contains:

- A constructor that calls the "Vehicle" constructor.
- "openBoot()" displays a message that the car boot is opened.

3. Bike Class

The "Bike" class also inherits from "Vehicle".

class Bike : public Vehicle

It contains:

- A constructor that calls the "Vehicle" constructor.
- "helmetReminder()" displays a reminder to wear a helmet.

Main Function

Two objects are created:

Car car("MH12AB1234");
Bike bike("MH12CD5678");

The program then:

1. Starts the car.
2. Opens the car boot.
3. Starts the bike.
4. Displays the helmet reminder.

Output

Vehicle MH12AB1234 started
Car boot opened
Vehicle MH12CD5678 started
Please wear a helmet

OOP Concept Used

Hierarchical Inheritance

              Vehicle
             /       \
            /         \
          Car         Bike

Both "Car" and "Bike" inherit common functionality from the "Vehicle" class while having their own specific functions.
